  "behavior": [
    "Private CellIndex now owns its real [i32;10] inline buffer, optional unique heap pointer and logical length in native field order. Existing public LocalArray<T,N> and its SmallVec representation are untouched.",
    "The three fields carry real storage and ownership state, with no arbitrary padding. Measured LP64 size56/alignment8/offsets0,40,48 produce actual CellRecord size64/head offset56 and MapNode size72, closing the previously recorded concrete byte gaps.",
    "Inline mode stores a null optional heap pointer and computes the current inline address on access. Ordinary Rust relocation therefore preserves valid storage; it never leaves a pointer referring to the previous object address.",
    "Index heap buffers use the existing header-backed common allocator policy corresponding to global native Standard allocation, independently of the CellFilter object arena. The original allocation layout survives logical size zero and can be freed by its saved provenance-bearing pointer without a capacity field.",
    "Explicit move construction steals heap storage and empties its source. Assignment copies inline source values and releases old heap storage, transfers heap into inline receivers, or swaps heap pointers so the empty donor retains the old receiver allocation. Copying an empty heap-backed donor produces an independent empty inline lookup index.",
    "Point construction starts with initialized coordinates; a callback panic drops the partially built owner and releases its heap allocation. Unrepresentable byte counts are rejected with the offending length before any raw allocation.",
    "Actual filter/map/range/hash paths all consume this CellIndex. For two real i32 cells and three objects, the native requests72/16/16/72/16 match measured Rust arena-consumption strides and the final concrete object node size.",
    "Shared immutable access and exclusive mutable slices preserve Send/Sync for the unique i32 owner. Existing filter and standalone Cell covariance/thread/move/destructor regressions remain green under strict-provenance Miri."
  ],
